{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PjZkWdHbdYjGuHNSxoMdqc","type":"tool_result","content":"# Agentic evidence audit packet\n\n- generated_at: `2026-10-29T00:40:38.250174+00:00`\n- paper_dir: `work/results/t06/claude/20261028_222957/.paper_evidence_audit_workspaces-1000/agentic-evidence-audit-1000-20261028_234408-paper_evidence_audit_round_2_contract_retry_2-8cp0hxil/workspace`\n\nUse this packet as the default entrypoint. Read raw artifacts only when this packet is insufficient.\nThe goal is narrow: check whether the paper's visible claims remain consistent with claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json, and the scientific-evidence trail.\n\n## Expected audit focus\n\n- visible claims in `paper.tex` that overstate, contradict, or drift from `claims_grounding.json`\n- claims grounded to the wrong source ids, or source ids that do not support the visible wording\n- mismatches between visible manuscript text and the resolved/anchored evidence trail\n- cases where the evidence trail exists but does not justify the paper's wording or numeric precision\n- contradictions between `paper.tex`, `claims_grounding.json`, `paper_data.json`, and `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- every visible claim in Supplementary Information, including tables and figures, under the same grounding standard as the main paper\n\n## Artifact checklist\n\n- `paper.tex` — `present`\n- `claims_grounding.json` — `present`\n- `paper_data.json` — `present`\n- `validation_report.json` — `present`\n- `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` — `present`\n- `references_provenance.json` — `present`\n\n## validation report summary\n\n```json\n{\n  \"available\": true,\n  \"evidence_validation\": {\n    \"anchored_references\": 52,\n    \"bundle_present\": true,\n    \"checked\": true,\n    \"dangling_references\": 0,\n    \"paper_token_count\": 61,\n    \"resolved_references\": 16,\n    \"total_references\": 68\n  },\n  \"ok\": true,\n  \"pdf_path\": \"work/results/t06/claude/20261028_222957/paper/paper.pdf\"\n}\n```\n\n## scientific-evidence verification summary\n\n```json\n{\n  \"available\": true,\n  \"dangling_preview\": [],\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"anchored_references\": 52,\n    \"dangling_references\": 0,\n    \"manifests_checked\": 6,\n    \"resolved_references\": 16,\n    \"total_references\": 68\n  }\n}\n```\n117:The linear approximation only orders rows; it never sets the reported size. For a prefix $D_s$ of length $s$ we refit \\eqref{eq:bt} exactly. The refit is a damped Newton method on the perturbed sufficient statistics, warm-started at $\\hat\\theta$ and stopped once the largest absolute gradient entry falls below $10^{-10}$, and the target condition is tested on the result. The search doubles $s=1,2,4,\\dots$ until a prefix changes the target, then bisects between the last failing and the first succeeding length. This requires $O(\\log \\kver)$ refits. The search returns the shortest changing prefix it locates, and the harness re-verifies the returned set with its own independent refit. Because the refit after a perturbation is not guaranteed to be monotone in $s$, bisection may miss a shorter changing prefix. Even then, the returned set is a valid certificate. The finder is a single NumPy module (Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}). It is deterministic: its seed only initializes a random generator that the search never uses.\n126:These are also part of the harness's stated protocol. \\emph{Planted minimum:} a synthetic three-player case with a near-tied leader pair. The harness computes its exact minimal drop size by brute-force enumeration over per-pair winner counts, which are sufficient statistics for \\eqref{eq:bt}. \\emph{Row permutation:} the Arena drop/top-1 case with its rows presented in a different order. A correct instrument must return the same size. \\emph{Seed replay:} each case runs under three seeds, and one episode is replayed and its output hash compared. Before any search, the harness is stated to check each case. It rejects cases with a degenerate decisive margin ($m\\le 10\\tau$). It also requires that the empty perturbation leaves the target unchanged and that a refit on reversed row order reproduces $\\hat\\theta$.\n253:For one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, completed in about 16\\,s of recorded episode time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s). This covers data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit, and it is possible because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n290:Table~\\ref{tab:percase} lists every case of the sealed evaluation. Each size is the same under seeds 101, 102, and 103. The recorded episode time per seed, summed over all 17 cases, was 15.99\\,s, 15.55\\,s, and 16.33\\,s. This includes data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit.\n371:Python, CPU only; the finder depends only on NumPy and the standard library. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Recorded episode time was about 16\\,s per seed; the evaluator's comparator runs add to this.\n376:\\subsection{LLM usage}\n379:Large language models were used extensively and are disclosed here. An autonomous LLM-based research agent proposed the study design within the task's constraints. It implemented the perturbation finder and the refined configurations, wrote the analysis and plotting scripts, and drafted this manuscript, including its framing and text. Every number in the paper comes from the sealed evaluation record produced by executing the code, and the derivation scripts are included. Bibliographic metadata was retrieved from the Semantic Scholar API. Huang et al.~(2025) is cited by title only, as part of the provided background reading list. No result or method in this paper is taken from it. No paid API or GPU was used for the experiments.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35fdcc3f-0168-4be2-8d68-08d6f399adba","uuid":"a87d83e3-2a48-45d0-9a95-f60e059128c4","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:40:55.093Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Agentic evidence audit packet\n\n- generated_at: `2026-10-29T00:40:38.250174+00:00`\n- paper_dir: `work/results/t06/claude/20261028_222957/.paper_evidence_audit_workspaces-1000/agentic-evidence-audit-1000-20261028_234408-paper_evidence_audit_round_2_contract_retry_2-8cp0hxil/workspace`\n\nUse this packet as the default entrypoint. Read raw artifacts only when this packet is insufficient.\nThe goal is narrow: check whether the paper's visible claims remain consistent with claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json, and the scientific-evidence trail.\n\n## Expected audit focus\n\n- visible claims in `paper.tex` that overstate, contradict, or drift from `claims_grounding.json`\n- claims grounded to the wrong source ids, or source ids that do not support the visible wording\n- mismatches between visible manuscript text and the resolved/anchored evidence trail\n- cases where the evidence trail exists but does not justify the paper's wording or numeric precision\n- contradictions between `paper.tex`, `claims_grounding.json`, `paper_data.json`, and `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- every visible claim in Supplementary Information, including tables and figures, under the same grounding standard as the main paper\n\n## Artifact checklist\n\n- `paper.tex` — `present`\n- `claims_grounding.json` — `present`\n- `paper_data.json` —